8
00:00:19,585 --> 00:00:22,037
every day without taking them offline.

9
00:00:22,472 --> 00:00:25,881
I'll cover three core patterns,
a framework for deciding when you

10
00:00:25,881 --> 00:00:29,868
are ready to cut over, and how to
turn these into reusable platform

11
00:00:29,869 --> 00:00:34,887
capability so you are not rebuilding
the wheel on the next migration.

12
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:41,683
Going to the next, agenda, here is what
I'll, cover in the next thirty minutes.

13
00:00:42,498 --> 00:00:45,703
We start with the problem,
why the instinct to do big

14
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:47,753
bang cut over is so dangerous.

15
00:00:48,199 --> 00:00:52,276
Then the three patterns, dual
write parallel run, bi-directional

16
00:00:52,287 --> 00:00:54,334
sync, change data capture.

17
00:00:55,565 --> 00:00:58,478
Each solves a different
version of migration problem.

18
00:00:58,793 --> 00:01:02,437
Then a framework for choosing the
right one and for knowing when,

19
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,795
when you are actually ready to flip.

20
00:01:05,572 --> 00:01:08,682
We close the organizational
shift, treating migration

21
00:01:08,688 --> 00:01:13,633
capability as a platform asset,
not a one of heroic effort.

22
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:18,112
Platform teams are now responsible
for most business critical

23
00:01:18,143 --> 00:01:19,992
migrations in the organization.

24
00:01:20,185 --> 00:01:25,266
Order management, healthcare claims,
CRM, these are systems where we--

25
00:01:25,784 --> 00:01:30,137
even a brief outage means real
revenue loss and compliance risk.

26
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:35,163
The patterns I'll show you up
today apply across all three.

27
00:01:35,663 --> 00:01:40,561
My Bath & Body Works OMS migration
from Radial to Manhattan Active Omni

28
00:01:40,612 --> 00:01:43,049
is one re-- one example I'll draw on.

29
00:01:43,523 --> 00:01:47,054
Hundred percent order of-- or
hundred percent of order traffic,

30
00:01:47,154 --> 00:01:48,903
zero tolerance for downtime

31
00:01:49,903 --> 00:01:52,064
Let's start with what goes wrong.

32
00:01:52,272 --> 00:01:54,657
Big bang cutovers feel
manageable in planning.

33
00:01:54,787 --> 00:01:59,560
One weekend, a rehearsed runbook, a
rollback plan, but they have concrete

34
00:01:59,595 --> 00:02:03,347
every risk into a single moment.

35
00:02:04,348 --> 00:02:05,447
Hidden data drift.

36
00:02:05,626 --> 00:02:09,718
Differences between old and new that
your test environment never reveals.

37
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:11,569
No real traffic validation.

38
00:02:11,916 --> 00:02:16,413
Your test data is never as messy,
as voluminous as production.

39
00:02:16,963 --> 00:02:17,885
Painful rollback.

40
00:02:17,913 --> 00:02:21,004
If anything goes wrong after records
are returned to the new system,

41
00:02:21,047 --> 00:02:22,990
unwinding is incredibly hard.

42
00:02:23,797 --> 00:02:25,388
Business-wide blast radius.

43
00:02:25,428 --> 00:02:29,690
Every user, every downstream
integration is affected at once.

44
00:02:29,727 --> 00:02:35,195
The core problem is you get one
shot with no live comparison, no

45
00:02:35,197 --> 00:02:39,691
gradual confidence building There
are three patterns that solve this,

46
00:02:39,955 --> 00:02:42,005
and they are not mutually exclusive.

47
00:02:42,492 --> 00:02:45,190
You'll often combine them
on a complex migration.

48
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:49,100
Pattern one, dual written--
dual write parallel run.

49
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:53,608
You run both systems simultaneously and
prove the new one before you trust it.

50
00:02:53,796 --> 00:02:56,322
Pattern two, bidirectional
synchronization.

51
00:02:56,332 --> 00:03:00,100
Both systems stay alive, consistent
while users migrate in waves.

52
00:03:00,152 --> 00:03:02,112
Pattern three, change data capture.

53
00:03:02,134 --> 00:03:06,505
You move data incrementally at the
database layer, no match window-- no

54
00:03:06,512 --> 00:03:08,966
batch windows, no loading the legacy app.

55
00:03:09,248 --> 00:03:14,299
Each one is worth building a reusable
in- infrastructure, not just solving

56
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,202
it once per project Dual write
parallel run is the most direct pattern.

57
00:03:20,626 --> 00:03:25,771
Every write goes to both the legacy system
and the new platform simultaneously.

58
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:28,155
The legacy system is a source of truth.

59
00:03:28,304 --> 00:03:30,368
Reads still come from there.

60
00:03:30,732 --> 00:03:35,204
You run the automated reconciliation layer
that compares outputs between the two.

61
00:03:35,609 --> 00:03:40,889
Once reconciliation consistently shows
zero divergence across a meaningful

62
00:03:40,914 --> 00:03:44,726
window of a real traffic, you start
flipping reads to the new platform

63
00:03:44,726 --> 00:03:46,472
gradually, not at all at once.

64
00:03:46,770 --> 00:03:50,456
The key design decision, what is
your reconciliation latency target?

65
00:03:50,893 --> 00:03:54,632
How do you surface divergences
to the engineering team?

66
00:03:54,689 --> 00:03:58,645
And critically, what's your
fast rollback path if reads re-

67
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:01,123
start returning wrong answers?

68
00:04:02,123 --> 00:04:04,137
The parallel run isn't a single flip.

69
00:04:04,191 --> 00:04:05,694
It's a fa- five phases.

70
00:04:05,789 --> 00:04:10,175
Shadow, the new platform receives
writes, but legacy serves all reads.

71
00:04:10,192 --> 00:04:12,730
You are proving the writes
without any risk to the user.

72
00:04:13,143 --> 00:04:18,643
Compare, you instrument the reconciliation
layer and fix every divergence found.

73
00:04:19,374 --> 00:04:21,222
This phase can take weeks.

74
00:04:21,270 --> 00:04:22,240
Don't skip it.

75
00:04:23,044 --> 00:04:27,936
Canary reads, you start routing a small
percentage of reads to the new platform.

76
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:30,544
This is your first real-time
traffic validation.

77
00:04:30,561 --> 00:04:34,418
Flip, the new platform
becomes a source of truth.

78
00:04:34,499 --> 00:04:36,538
Legacy is still alive as a fallback.

79
00:04:36,625 --> 00:04:40,466
Retire, once you are confident and
the fallback is never triggered

80
00:04:40,796 --> 00:04:42,211
and you decommission the legacy.

81
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:46,807
The key discipline, never rush
from compare to canary until your

82
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:50,014
divergence rate is at or near zero.

83
00:04:51,838 --> 00:04:56,576
Bidirectional sync is the right pattern
when you can't do a clean cut, when

84
00:04:56,735 --> 00:05:00,704
different s- teams or services will
be on different systems for months.

85
00:05:00,742 --> 00:05:02,790
The sync layer is the
heart of this pattern.

86
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:07,171
It listens to changes on both
sides and propagates them across.

87
00:05:07,203 --> 00:05:09,430
Both systems stay alive and consistent.

88
00:05:09,831 --> 00:05:14,422
Three things you must get right, conflict
resolution rules, which system wins on

89
00:05:14,424 --> 00:05:20,469
a field-by-field basis, loop prevention,
a changed sync with from A to B must

90
00:05:20,469 --> 00:05:24,658
not echo back A, and idempotent writes.

91
00:05:24,819 --> 00:05:28,339
Retries and replays mu- must be safe.

92
00:05:28,622 --> 00:05:31,239
We'll go deeper on all
three on the next slide

93
00:05:32,239 --> 00:05:36,976
These are design rules for non-negotiable
in a bidirectional sync architecture.

94
00:05:37,436 --> 00:05:40,628
Resolve contracts- conflicts
de- deterministically.

95
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:46,437
Define per-field ownership or use
last write wins with timestamps.

96
00:05:46,795 --> 00:05:48,732
We'll figure it out at runtime.

97
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:50,616
Not-- This is not a strategy.

98
00:05:51,304 --> 00:05:52,609
Prevent echo loops.

99
00:05:52,746 --> 00:05:55,246
Every change must carry an origin tag.

100
00:05:55,337 --> 00:05:59,050
The sync layer checks this tag
and refuses to propagate a change

101
00:05:59,065 --> 00:06:00,458
back to where it came from.

102
00:06:01,036 --> 00:06:03,555
Make every write idempotent.

103
00:06:03,849 --> 00:06:07,087
The sync will replay events on failures.

104
00:06:07,115 --> 00:06:10,816
If a replay creates a duplicate
record, your data is corrupt.

105
00:06:10,890 --> 00:06:13,145
Use upsert semantics everywhere.

106
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:15,215
Preserve ordering.

107
00:06:15,912 --> 00:06:20,008
Apply changes per entity in event order.

108
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:27,728
Out of order applies will leave you--
your data in an inconsistent state Change

109
00:06:27,728 --> 00:06:29,842
data capture works at a database layer.

110
00:06:29,899 --> 00:06:33,402
It reads the transaction log
directly, not the application layer.

111
00:06:33,639 --> 00:06:37,019
This matters because it means zero
load on the legacy application.

112
00:06:37,029 --> 00:06:37,993
You are not polling.

113
00:06:38,029 --> 00:06:39,675
You are not running batch queries.

114
00:06:39,702 --> 00:06:41,122
You are just streaming the log.

115
00:06:41,824 --> 00:06:43,805
The latency improvements is real.

116
00:06:43,846 --> 00:06:46,459
Batch window ETL introduces hours of lag.

117
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:49,085
CDC pipelines operate in
a range of milliseconds to

118
00:06:49,086 --> 00:06:50,633
seconds for most workloads.

119
00:06:51,462 --> 00:06:55,411
On the Forrester citation, this refers
to their research on event-driven

120
00:06:55,411 --> 00:06:59,324
architecture, adoption, and latency
benchmarks available publicly.

121
00:06:59,868 --> 00:07:02,884
If you want specific report,
happy to share it afterwards.

122
00:07:03,884 --> 00:07:05,923
Here is the anatomy of CDC pipeline.

123
00:07:05,932 --> 00:07:09,740
The CDC connector reads the
transaction log of a legacy database.

124
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:13,483
No schema changes, no application
instrumental-- instrumentation need.

125
00:07:13,496 --> 00:07:17,372
It publishes change events to a
durable, replayable event stream.

126
00:07:17,711 --> 00:07:20,758
Consumers on the other end are decoupled.

127
00:07:20,859 --> 00:07:24,885
The new platform, a search index,
analytics systems, they all read

128
00:07:25,055 --> 00:07:26,830
from the same stream independently.

129
00:07:27,354 --> 00:07:28,536
Three operating principles.

130
00:07:28,546 --> 00:07:32,836
Start with full snapshot before you
start streaming incremental changes.

131
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:37,069
Handle schema changes as versioned
events, not breaking changes.

132
00:07:37,335 --> 00:07:39,728
And monitor consumer lag end-to-end.

133
00:07:39,808 --> 00:07:42,143
Lag is your migration's heartbeat.

134
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:46,203
If lag grows, you have a
problem Here is how to match

135
00:07:46,204 --> 00:07:47,990
the pattern to your migration.

136
00:07:48,091 --> 00:07:52,566
Dual write parallel run is the best when
you need to prove the new system on real

137
00:07:52,566 --> 00:07:54,794
production traffic before cutting over.

138
00:07:55,245 --> 00:07:58,604
The legacy app needs to
write to both systems, which

139
00:07:58,606 --> 00:08:00,122
means an application change.

140
00:08:00,653 --> 00:08:03,763
Bidirectional sync is best
for long coexistences.

141
00:08:04,849 --> 00:08:09,793
When you are migrating in waves, both
systems need to serve real users.

142
00:08:09,835 --> 00:08:12,366
The complexity cost is
a sync layer itself.

143
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:16,261
Change data capture is a best for high
volume data movement, especially when

144
00:08:16,262 --> 00:08:18,082
you can't touch the legacy application.

145
00:08:18,563 --> 00:08:21,274
The risk is lag and schema drift.

146
00:08:21,495 --> 00:08:26,364
Most real migrations combine of
t- two of these: CDC for the data

147
00:08:26,366 --> 00:08:29,672
layer, dual write and bidirectional
sync for the application layer.

148
00:08:31,308 --> 00:08:36,252
Before you cut over, you need to score
your readiness across five dimensions.

149
00:08:36,361 --> 00:08:37,088
Latency.

150
00:08:37,203 --> 00:08:39,543
How fresh is data across both systems?

151
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:42,815
If the new platform is running
stale, users will notice.

152
00:08:43,067 --> 00:08:43,862
Reliability.

153
00:08:44,155 --> 00:08:46,871
Does your sync survive
failure and replay correctly?

154
00:08:47,147 --> 00:08:49,897
Chaos test is this before cut over.

155
00:08:50,270 --> 00:08:51,185
Bidirectional.

156
00:08:51,535 --> 00:08:56,297
Can changes flow both ways
without loops or data corruption?

157
00:08:57,313 --> 00:08:58,060
Reliability.

158
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:01,699
Does your sync survive failure
and re- replay correctly?

159
00:09:01,718 --> 00:09:04,781
Chaos test this even before cut over.

160
00:09:05,294 --> 00:09:06,852
And, scalability.

161
00:09:07,298 --> 00:09:11,491
Have you load tested at peak
production volume, not just an average?

162
00:09:12,135 --> 00:09:13,098
Migration safety.

163
00:09:13,115 --> 00:09:17,045
Do you have a verified rollback
path, a no-go checklist, and

164
00:09:17,045 --> 00:09:18,538
a step-by-step cut over plan?

165
00:09:19,405 --> 00:09:23,438
If you can't score all five with
confidence, you are not ready.

166
00:09:23,530 --> 00:09:29,494
Set a date when you can The shift I want
to leave you in is with the-- with this

167
00:09:29,744 --> 00:09:34,975
organizational, not just technical, is
when every migration team builds their

168
00:09:34,992 --> 00:09:39,832
own sync and reconciliation from scratch,
you get inconsistent safety guarantees,

169
00:09:39,870 --> 00:09:44,320
ad hoc rollback plans, and migrations
that are high-stress events every time.

170
00:09:44,769 --> 00:09:49,487
When you build these three patterns
as platform capability, dual-write

171
00:09:49,497 --> 00:09:53,548
bi-directional sync CDC, teams
can consume proven primitives.

172
00:09:54,375 --> 00:09:56,189
Readiness gets scored consistently.

173
00:09:56,251 --> 00:09:59,454
Cutovers become routine engineering
work, not heroic weekends.

174
00:09:59,493 --> 00:10:03,582
The investment pays back on the
second migration and every one after

